  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1027 PM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nTroughing along the West Coast and ridging across the Southeast will \nkeep eastern Utah and western Colorado under southwesterly flow \nthrough the end of the work week. This will pull in drier air, \ngradually reducing precipitation chances through the end of the week \nas surface level moisture is scoured out. That said, chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms will remain each afternoon, primarily \nacross the higher terrain. Enough surface moisture is expected to \nstick around Tuesday to keep the potential for heavy rain around, as \nwell the potential for flash flooding in vulnerable locations. As \ndrier air continues to push in, the main threat with storms will \ntransition to that of gusty outflow winds. Lightning will be a \nthreat throughout the period with any storm. Temperatures will warm \nday by day as we dry out, reaching near-normal values by the end of \nthe work-week. \n\nA strong closed low is forecast to drop out of the Gulf of Alaska by \nthe weekend, forcing the West Coast trough to retrograde slightly. \nThis will allow the Southeast ridge to also expand westward, and \nultimately the flow over eastern Utah and western Colorado will \nshift to a more southerly direction. This will once again tap into \ndeeper monsoonal moisture, bringing a return to wetter and cooler \nconditions. Ensemble and deterministic guidance does remain in \ndecent agreement regarding a plume of above normal PWATs reaching \neastern Utah and western Colorado by Saturday, but there does remain \nsome uncertainty. Changes with the tropical system activity in the \nPacific could substantially alter this forecast, so be sure to stay \ntuned for updates on how this weekend's forecast develops.",
